 techniques in wilderness  first aid  all roads seem to lead through conrad  anchor  so how did conrad sort of get involved  in this whole expedition  it's funny that you put it that way  because i mean i've known conrad for  over 30 years but one of the things that  that conrad has been really supportive  of is  making the outdoors more accessible to  more people you know i think that he has  recognized his personal privilege but  also  you know perhaps the responsibility of  making sure that the outdoors is more  accessible to more people one of the  things that i really you know respect  him for is  he kept his role exclusively advisory  you know he offered support and  encouragement you know when  someone needed to be  persuaded to support the expedition he  put in a good word for us but he allowed  this expedition to happen organically  philip henderson did all the heavy  lifting you know and  and this ultimately made this an  expedition that  was 100  person of color led  how did they go about putting together a  team i mean you can know a lot of people  but climbing mount everest  is sort of a whole another project so  what how did they go about doing it well  you know and it's it's it's really  interesting because you know there are a  lot of climbing festivals you know and  after a while you know you kind of have  this  camaraderie circle because inevitably  you know when you see a black climber at  an event like this and you're a black  climber you're going to connect  you know you're you're going to  gravitate towards one another but  then again of course you know  who has the skill and expertise to do  vertical ice climbing  it's a very verified skill set you know  so there's not very many people who can  do that so you you know start  asking around so you know who's good at  ice climbing  coming up  phil henderson finds an ice climber  we'll meet one of the members of the  full circle expedition  and learn how he went from growing up in  brooklyn  to scaling frozen waterfalls  [Music]  putting together a high altitude  climbing team is a tricky business  first you have to have people with  specialized skills  who are extremely physically fit and who  can work together under really intense  pressure  and you have to be ready to trust them  with your life  and in dom mullins phil found one of his  guys  don is an amazing character and um  he's you know one of the few climbers  that i didn't know before we put the  expedition together  excuse me here because i just got my  food served so i'll be sipping a little  soup while we're talking  this is dom when james reached him dom  was in the himalayas training for the  climbing season  dom took the winding road to becoming a  mountaineer he grew up in brooklyn not  many mountains around brooklyn and he  enlisted in the army after high school  at the age of 19  he was  a recruit in the u.s army on september  11th 2001  and he literally  you know in basic training was was  plunged into  the iraq war  um and was deployed originally to new  york city to patrol the site of the fall  of the 10 point towers  but he was actually among the very first  ground troops that were sent to  baghdad and fallujah  after his discharge dom became an  anti-war activist he worked as a staffer  in the u.s senate and then he earned a  phd at the city university of new york  studying sociology  his military network led him to a group  called veterans expeditions a non-profit  that organizes outdoor trips for vets  dom started climbing mountains with the  group in colorado and he realized there  was a whole world he knew nothing about  i didn't have an understanding of the  ceiling basically  of this sort of activity  something that i've fallen in love with  thereafter which is ice climbing i  didn't even know that that existed at  the time  you know i grew up in brooklyn i never  even seen a frozen waterfall much less  thought about the considered the fact  that waterfalls freeze you know what 